WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.668
-Our next guest is a very funny
actress and singer

00:00:02.668 --> 00:00:05.237
who you know from her work
on "Saturday Night Live"

00:00:05.237 --> 00:00:06.139
and "The Goldbergs."

00:00:06.139 --> 00:00:07.440
She stars in "American Auto,"

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:09.142
which airs Tuesday nights
here on NBC.

00:00:09.142 --> 00:00:10.710
Let's take a look.
-There's the, uh,

00:00:10.710 --> 00:00:12.945
official ribbon-cutting ceremony
at the end of the day.

00:00:12.945 --> 00:00:14.513
-God, these townies
really love a chance

00:00:14.513 --> 00:00:15.682
to celebrate, don't they?

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:17.083
-Well, this is the first factory
to open there

00:00:17.083 --> 00:00:17.950
since the recession.

00:00:17.950 --> 00:00:19.185
It's a lot of new jobs.

00:00:19.185 --> 00:00:20.887
They -- They want to express
their gratitude.

00:00:20.887 --> 00:00:22.088
-Well, it's nice.

00:00:22.088 --> 00:00:23.623
The public never really liked me

00:00:23.623 --> 00:00:25.191
when I was CEO
of a drug company.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.059
I don't know why.
-Probably the opioid crisis.

00:00:27.059 --> 00:00:28.261
-Maybe.

00:00:28.261 --> 00:00:30.363
-Or the price gouging
on prescription drugs.

00:00:30.363 --> 00:00:32.365
-You know what?
I was a girl boss.

00:00:32.365 --> 00:00:34.033
That's very tough
for some people.

00:00:34.033 --> 00:00:35.501
-Please welcome back to the show

00:00:35.501 --> 00:00:37.837
our very good friend
Ana Gasteyer.

00:00:37.837 --> 00:00:39.238
How are you, Ana?

00:00:39.238 --> 00:00:41.807
-Hi, friend.
How are you?

00:00:41.807 --> 00:00:43.076
-I'm good.

00:00:43.076 --> 00:00:46.178
I was lucky to see you
right before the holidays.

00:00:46.178 --> 00:00:47.647
We were both robust.

00:00:47.647 --> 00:00:49.148
We were both...
-We were.

00:00:49.148 --> 00:00:51.717
-...feeling healthy,
looking well.

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:53.219
-We were hale.

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.656
-And you also went through
what I'm going through now.

00:00:56.656 --> 00:00:59.692
You had a COVID issue
at your home.

00:00:59.692 --> 00:01:01.460
-Yes, we were felled by COVID.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.398
We spent the holidays in a room,
and we're on the other side.

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:09.134
And now I feel like Superman,
because I'm super boosted.

00:01:09.134 --> 00:01:10.870
-Super boosted.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.106
You did get to go out --

00:01:13.106 --> 00:01:16.008
You did some shows
before you guys got hit.

00:01:16.008 --> 00:01:19.378
You did some live shows on your
"Sugar and Booze" tour, correct?

00:01:19.378 --> 00:01:20.746
-That's exactly right.

00:01:20.746 --> 00:01:24.383
I toured the COVID belt,

00:01:24.383 --> 00:01:29.655
and then I literally finished

00:01:29.655 --> 00:01:34.059
everything I was supposed to
be doing on December 20th.

00:01:34.059 --> 00:01:36.829
And I flew home,
and I felt crappy.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:39.398
Tested myself, and my son and I
were both sick.

00:01:39.398 --> 00:01:40.934
So we canceled Christmas,

00:01:40.934 --> 00:01:45.471
and I'm really caught up on all
shows relevant to 13-year-olds.

00:01:45.471 --> 00:01:46.805
-That's wonderful.

00:01:46.805 --> 00:01:51.144
Now, did you love being up
and doing live shows again

00:01:51.144 --> 00:01:52.545
before you were felled?

00:01:52.545 --> 00:01:54.881
-Oh, yeah, it was fantastic.
Of course, I haven't done --

00:01:54.881 --> 00:01:56.749
These were shows that pushed
from 2020.

00:01:56.749 --> 00:01:58.785
So, no, it was incredible,
actually,

00:01:58.785 --> 00:02:01.253
to be out in the world.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:02.522
You know, it was exciting.

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:05.058
I was in Kentucky
for the tornadoes.

00:02:05.058 --> 00:02:07.393
That was exciting.
-Oh, my goodness.

00:02:07.393 --> 00:02:10.463
-Yeah, it was terrifying
and very sad and upsetting.

00:02:10.463 --> 00:02:16.736
But I dressed in all my clothes
and I laid in the bathtub.

00:02:16.736 --> 00:02:19.305
So that was cool.
-Alright.

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:21.407
You've had a very -- Since
the last time I've seen you,

00:02:21.407 --> 00:02:22.742
a lot has gone on.

00:02:22.742 --> 00:02:25.912
And a lot has gone on since
you first started working

00:02:25.912 --> 00:02:28.214
on this show "American Auto."

00:02:28.214 --> 00:02:31.484
This is about two years
in the making, correct?

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:33.219
-This show was --

00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:36.088
I was supposed to do this pilot
the week of the shutdown --

00:02:36.088 --> 00:02:37.957
the big original shutdown.

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:40.626
So, yeah,
we ended up doing the --

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.628
I mean, you know, I went out
to L.A., did my fittings.

00:02:42.628 --> 00:02:43.930
They were supposed
to do a table read.

00:02:43.930 --> 00:02:47.267
That got canceled,
and everybody ran home.

00:02:47.267 --> 00:02:50.135
And I thought,
"Oh, well, there goes my pilot."

00:02:50.135 --> 00:02:53.139
And then we filmed
the first season last July.

00:02:53.139 --> 00:02:54.740
So July through, you know, fall.

00:02:54.740 --> 00:02:56.275
So, yeah.
So here we are.

00:02:56.275 --> 00:02:59.178
So it was definitely like
a long-fermented fruit,

00:02:59.178 --> 00:03:00.680
if you will.

00:03:00.680 --> 00:03:02.682
-So, as we can see
from the clip,

00:03:02.682 --> 00:03:04.851
you play the CEO
of an auto company.

00:03:04.851 --> 00:03:07.053
And we are living in a time

00:03:07.053 --> 00:03:09.321
of great television shows
about corporate culture,

00:03:09.321 --> 00:03:12.057
and yet I know you and I have
a very similar background

00:03:12.057 --> 00:03:14.961
of not spending any time
actually in corporate culture.

00:03:14.961 --> 00:03:17.663
Do you enjoy pretending to know
what you're talking about?

00:03:17.663 --> 00:03:20.566
-Very much so.
And I like to talk to people.

00:03:20.566 --> 00:03:22.968
I mean, we've had overlords,
I would argue.

00:03:22.968 --> 00:03:24.204
We've had network --

00:03:24.204 --> 00:03:26.739
-We have had our share
of overlords.

00:03:26.739 --> 00:03:28.407
-[ Laughs ]

00:03:28.407 --> 00:03:31.009
But, yeah, no, I mean,
I think you're right.

00:03:31.009 --> 00:03:32.979
It is a moment, but there are
not a lot of comedies

00:03:32.979 --> 00:03:34.380
that are taking down
corporate America.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:37.450
So it is a very classic
NBC half-hour workplace comedy,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:38.784
but it is from the point of view

00:03:38.784 --> 00:03:40.720
of these, you know, dodos
making the decisions.

00:03:40.720 --> 00:03:42.621
And I'm not saying
all of corporate America

00:03:42.621 --> 00:03:43.790
is full of dodos.

00:03:43.790 --> 00:03:46.158
Especially our overlords
are not dodos at all.

00:03:46.158 --> 00:03:47.627
They're super smart.
-So sharp.

00:03:47.627 --> 00:03:50.630
I mean, yeah, smart as a whip,
the people we're dealing with.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:52.198
-No, no, no,
they're super sharp.

00:03:52.198 --> 00:03:54.033
Yeah, yeah, yeah,
but, yeah, so --

00:03:54.033 --> 00:03:57.102
but my character
comes from big pharma.

00:03:57.102 --> 00:04:00.339
She came in to run
an automotive company,

00:04:00.339 --> 00:04:01.874
in part because she's a woman.

00:04:01.874 --> 00:04:03.742
They needed to diversify
a little bit, I think.

00:04:03.742 --> 00:04:06.945
And she doesn't know how to
drive, which is problematic.

00:04:06.945 --> 00:04:08.547
She's not big on cars.

00:04:08.547 --> 00:04:12.517
But, you know, she's good at
bossing people around, so --

00:04:12.517 --> 00:04:15.488
-What was your first car?

00:04:15.488 --> 00:04:17.590
-Datsun B210.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:20.659
Try not to be too impressed.

00:04:20.659 --> 00:04:22.595
Not unlike a beer can
with wheels.

00:04:22.595 --> 00:04:24.730
It had almost a go-kart quality.

00:04:24.730 --> 00:04:26.398
[ Laughs ]

00:04:26.398 --> 00:04:27.834
-I had one of those.

00:04:27.834 --> 00:04:32.804
I had a Renault Le Car, which
was just like a planter box.

00:04:32.804 --> 00:04:34.673
I feel like that's what
you should use it for, yeah.

00:04:34.673 --> 00:04:36.843
-Yeah, I clipped a bus --
an actual bus --

00:04:36.843 --> 00:04:38.610
and it was as though
the Jolly Green Giant

00:04:38.610 --> 00:04:39.645
had turned it on its side

00:04:39.645 --> 00:04:40.779
and just stepped
in the middle of it.

00:04:40.779 --> 00:04:43.315
It's sort of a miracle
that I survived.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:46.619
That was my first car,
and I learned --

00:04:46.619 --> 00:04:49.521
My mom insisted that I learn
to drive stick.

00:04:49.521 --> 00:04:51.624
That was something --
But I grew up on Capitol Hill,

00:04:51.624 --> 00:04:54.059
as you may recall,
literally like on Capitol Hill,

00:04:54.059 --> 00:04:56.395
like three blocks
from the Capitol building,

00:04:56.395 --> 00:04:59.398
and my mom taught me
to drive a stick shift car

00:04:59.398 --> 00:05:01.333
during rush hour
on Capitol Hill.

00:05:01.333 --> 00:05:03.269
It was absolutely terrifying.

00:05:03.269 --> 00:05:06.238
So, I did my time, but I don't
drive very much anymore.

00:05:06.238 --> 00:05:09.508
-Well, then it's very fitting
that you play a CEO

00:05:09.508 --> 00:05:13.046
of an automobile company
on a new sitcom.

